WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.880 --> 00:00:05.210
Box office victory is a tricky thing to capture,
whether you're a bold new idea with some heavy

00:00:05.210 --> 00:00:08.960
star power or the latest installment of a
reliable franchise.

00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:14.200
Quite a few 2019 movies with big names and
big budgets have received painfully disappointing

00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:18.920
thumbs downs from critics and audiences alike,
and no genre seems immune.

00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:22.020
Here are 2019's biggest box office bombs.

00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:27.520
In spite of mostly positive reviews, Missing
Link missed pretty spectacularly at the box office.

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:33.120
A yeti named Susan, voiced by Zach Galifianakis
is found in the Pacific Northwest by Sir Lionel

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:35.360
Frost, voiced by Hugh Jackman.

00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:40.730
Susan, Frost, and Frost's old romantic partner
Adelina Fortnight, voiced by Zoe Saldana embark

00:00:40.730 --> 00:00:44.100
on a quest to unite Susan with other yeti
in the Himalayas.

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:48.880
While it got thumbs-ups from critics, Missing
Link didn't just do badly, it made history.

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:53.080
According to the website Cartoon Brew, Missing
Link had the lowest opening ever for a film

00:00:53.081 --> 00:00:58.850
released in more than 3,200 theaters with
its opening weekend take of $5.8 million.

00:00:58.850 --> 00:01:03.030
It was also the lowest grossing release for
the stop-motion animation studio Laika, whose

00:01:03.030 --> 00:01:09.290
first film, 2009's Coraline, enjoyed a worldwide
gross of $75.3 million, compared to Missing

00:01:09.290 --> 00:01:11.700
Link's $16.2 million.

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:15.580
"Sir Lionel, have you ever wanted something
so bad it hurt inside?

00:01:15.580 --> 00:01:18.180
Like gas, but sadder?"

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:22.939
Laika has taken modern animation in creatively
satisfying new directions in the past, but

00:01:22.940 --> 00:01:24.740
they may actually be the problem.

00:01:24.740 --> 00:01:29.680
Since Coraline, each successive new movie
from the studio has performed worse than its predecessor.

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:33.860
Keeping this in mind and paying attention
to those few critics who panned Missing Link

00:01:33.860 --> 00:01:36.640
paints the picture of a studio that's lost
its edge.

00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:39.760
Here's hoping they can get it back together
for their next release.

00:01:40.220 --> 00:01:44.660
In a universe where dolls are tailor-made
for specific children, Uglyville is the receptacle

00:01:44.670 --> 00:01:48.689
for the ones too strange to survive the Institute
of Perfection's standards.

00:01:48.689 --> 00:01:53.759
In UglyDolls, one of the toys dumped in Uglyville,
named Moxy, voiced by Kelly Clarkson, leads

00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:57.240
her friends to the outside world to find out
what exists beyond their town.

00:01:57.240 --> 00:01:58.940
Sounds like an okay premise, right?

00:01:58.940 --> 00:02:02.740
Anthropomorphic dolls, singing, plenty of
laughs, a good message for kids that being

00:02:02.740 --> 00:02:04.060
different isn't bad.

00:02:04.060 --> 00:02:05.460
It's what makes you unique!

00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:06.900
What could go wrong?

00:02:06.900 --> 00:02:09.780
According to the critics, very little went
right.

00:02:09.790 --> 00:02:14.030
UglyDolls may say it's about celebrating difference,
but critics thought it was just another helping

00:02:14.030 --> 00:02:17.560
of the same old stuff, with a boring plot
that couldn't even get to the level of being

00:02:17.560 --> 00:02:19.040
interestingly bland.

00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:22.880
Another common complaint is that, while we
all know movies aimed at kids work hard for

00:02:22.880 --> 00:02:27.440
the merchandise revenue, UglyDolls seemed
to be doing nothing but advertising its merch,

00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:30.700
in this case a toy line that launched back
in 2001.

00:02:30.700 --> 00:02:34.920
Parents seemed to pick up on all this, and
as a result, UglyDolls pulled in a worldwide

00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:40.620
gross of $19.7 million, not even half its
budget of $45 million.

00:02:41.220 --> 00:02:45.920
The delightful coming-of-age comedy Booksmart,
the directorial debut of actress Olivia Wilde,

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:50.060
is exactly the kind of film that modern audiences
say that they want, but that Hollywood just

00:02:50.060 --> 00:02:51.140
won't give them.

00:02:51.140 --> 00:02:55.620
It's not a superhero film, a sci-fi adventure,
or an adaptation of an existing property;

00:02:55.620 --> 00:02:59.970
it's smart, funny, and is led by a pair of
talented young women in Kaitlyn Dever and

00:02:59.970 --> 00:03:01.200
Beanie Feldstein.

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:06.300
Comedic heavies including Lisa Kudrow, Jason
Sudeikis, and Will Forte stocked its supporting cast.

00:03:06.300 --> 00:03:10.080
It even had the seal of approval from critics
who absolutely loved the movie.

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:12.760
Audiences, however, could not have cared less.

00:03:12.760 --> 00:03:15.080
"It's basically a popularity contest, he's
useless."

00:03:15.080 --> 00:03:17.540
Sure, the film opened to some stiff competition.

00:03:17.540 --> 00:03:21.900
Disney's live-action Aladdin opened in the
same weekend, John Wick: Chapter 3, Parabellum

00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:25.850
dropped the weekend before, and Avengers:
Endgame was still chugging along toward eventual

00:03:25.850 --> 00:03:27.260
world domination.

00:03:27.260 --> 00:03:31.260
Booksmart wasn't really aiming for the same
audiences, though, going for a crowd with

00:03:31.260 --> 00:03:35.420
a preference for well-made, thoughtful, original
fare over big-budget tent poles.

00:03:35.420 --> 00:03:36.340
The problem?

00:03:36.340 --> 00:03:40.180
That crowd simply failed to show up, maybe
because they're content to wait for smaller,

00:03:40.180 --> 00:03:43.540
more cerebral flicks to show up on streaming
services.

00:03:43.540 --> 00:03:48.010
If this describes you, take note: if you'd
like Hollywood to make more films like Booksmart,

00:03:48.010 --> 00:03:51.000
you may want to see the ones they do give
us in the theater.

00:03:51.540 --> 00:03:56.040
It wasn't at all clear whether audiences even
wanted another installment in the Shaft series,

00:03:56.040 --> 00:03:59.920
which fielded three entries starring Richard
Roundtree as the legendary private eye who

00:03:59.920 --> 00:04:04.530
would risk his neck for his brother man in
the '70s, and was revitalized with a 2000

00:04:04.530 --> 00:04:08.100
entry starring Samuel L. Jackson as the legendary
P.I.'s nephew.

00:04:08.100 --> 00:04:12.820
Nearly 20 years later, Jackson and Roundtree
returned for a multi-generational teamup which

00:04:12.820 --> 00:04:16.010
also included the talented Jessie T. Usher
as J.J.

00:04:16.010 --> 00:04:18.300
Shaft, the son of Jackson's character.

00:04:18.300 --> 00:04:22.880
Rather than giving moviegoers an updated take
on the franchise, however, Shaft 2019 went

00:04:22.880 --> 00:04:25.760
backwards in all the worst possible ways.

00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:30.180
Critics found the film to be jaw-droppingly
regressive, with misogynistic and homophobic

00:04:30.190 --> 00:04:35.600
streaks each a mile wide; its outdated aesthetic
wasn't helped by a flimsy, predictable plot

00:04:35.600 --> 00:04:39.620
and a troubling reliance on stereotypes that
that haven't played well for decades.

00:04:39.620 --> 00:04:43.410
Shaft is a case in which a film's failure
can't be chalked up to anything other than

00:04:43.410 --> 00:04:45.320
what's right there on the surface.

00:04:45.320 --> 00:04:50.160
The flick was simply an awful, borderline
depressing waste of time, so bad that studio

00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:53.100
New Line dumped the film straight to Netflix
internationally.

00:04:53.100 --> 00:04:57.400
It failed to even make back its meager $30
million dollar budget, proving that, contrary

00:04:57.400 --> 00:05:02.270
to a rightfully widely-held popular belief,
it takes more than Samuel L. Jackson to make

00:05:02.270 --> 00:05:03.940
an awesome, profitable picture.

00:05:04.300 --> 00:05:08.160
Terminator: Dark Fate seemingly had all of
the ingredients to successfully resurrect

00:05:08.160 --> 00:05:12.430
the long-floundering franchise: the return
of James Cameron as a producer, the involvement

00:05:12.430 --> 00:05:17.131
of Deadpool director Tim Miller, Linda Hamilton
saddling up once again as Sarah Connor, and

00:05:17.140 --> 00:05:20.040
a slew of talented young actors filling out
the cast.

00:05:20.040 --> 00:05:24.160
Ignoring the three increasingly ridiculous
installments that came after Terminator 2:

00:05:24.160 --> 00:05:28.960
Judgment Day, the flick even garnered mostly
positive reviews, so how did it become perhaps

00:05:28.960 --> 00:05:31.100
the biggest money loser of the year?

00:05:31.100 --> 00:05:32.440
"Talk.

00:05:33.640 --> 00:05:35.440
Talk fast."

00:05:39.040 --> 00:05:40.300
"You first."

00:05:40.580 --> 00:05:45.200
There's no easy answer, but one thing seems
certain: Dark Fate failed to garner any interest

00:05:45.200 --> 00:05:49.180
whatsoever among younger moviegoers, many
of whom weren't even born when Terminator

00:05:49.180 --> 00:05:52.280
2 became a cultural phenomenon in 1991.

00:05:52.280 --> 00:05:56.840
It could very well be that the older fans
who loved that film and its 1984 predecessor

00:05:56.840 --> 00:06:00.639
simply failed to pass that love down to the
new generation, whose familiarity with the

00:06:00.639 --> 00:06:05.930
franchise mostly began and ended with three
critically reviled movies, 2003's Terminator

00:06:05.930 --> 00:06:11.510
3: Rise of the Machines, 2009's Terminator:
Salvation, and the relatively recent Terminator:

00:06:11.510 --> 00:06:15.800
Genisys from 2015, that Dark Fate completely
ignored.

00:06:15.800 --> 00:06:20.240
In any event, the aptly titled Dark Fate,
which was intended to kick start a new trilogy,

00:06:20.240 --> 00:06:24.020
will almost certainly end up being the final
nail in the coffin of the franchise.

00:06:24.020 --> 00:06:27.980
The filmmakers can at least take solace in
the fact that Terminator went out on a high

00:06:27.980 --> 00:06:31.440
note, but this time, it sure looks like he...won't
be back.

00:06:31.449 --> 00:06:32.980
Okay, sorry about that one.

00:06:32.980 --> 00:06:34.100
We kinda had to.

00:06:34.660 --> 00:06:38.600
Waiting decades to make a sequel to a beloved
film is always a tricky endeavor.

00:06:38.600 --> 00:06:43.340
Just ask the filmmakers behind Blade Runner
2049, a very good movie that underperformed

00:06:43.350 --> 00:06:45.300
severely at the box office.

00:06:45.300 --> 00:06:50.300
Doctor Sleep, adapted from Stephen King's
2013 sequel to his classic novel The Shining,

00:06:50.300 --> 00:06:54.840
positioned itself as a continuation of Stanley
Kubrick's much-adored 1980 film version of

00:06:54.840 --> 00:06:56.000
the original novel.

00:06:56.000 --> 00:07:00.820
And while this certainly seems like a sure-fire
formula for box office gold, the movie's shockingly

00:07:00.830 --> 00:07:06.020
poor performance illustrated that, decent
reviews aside, audiences were simply uninterested

00:07:06.020 --> 00:07:10.480
in picking up with an adult Danny Torrance
nearly four decades after Kubrick's film hit

00:07:10.480 --> 00:07:12.880
screens, even if he's played by Ewan McGregor.

00:07:12.880 --> 00:07:16.390
To be fair, Doctor Sleep debuted during a
Veterans' Day weekend during which it seemed

00:07:16.390 --> 00:07:20.120
that nobody could be bothered to go to the
movies at all, but as was the case with the

00:07:20.120 --> 00:07:23.960
Terminator: Dark Fate, it seemed that younger
audiences simply didn't give a rip about a

00:07:23.960 --> 00:07:28.500
sequel to a movie that probably came out before
their parents were even old enough to see it.

00:07:28.780 --> 00:07:33.340
With September's It: Chapter Two failing to
hit the heights of its 2017 predecessor, it

00:07:33.350 --> 00:07:37.830
could also be that the Stephen King Renaissance
is winding down, adding up to a perhaps the

00:07:37.830 --> 00:07:39.780
biggest mid-budget flop of the year.

00:07:40.440 --> 00:07:44.180
One thing can be said for the director of
the latest Charlie's Angels reboot, Elizabeth

00:07:44.200 --> 00:07:46.840
Banks: she got right out in front of the movie's
failure.

00:07:46.840 --> 00:07:50.660
In an interview shortly before the flick's
release, she gave her opinion that if the

00:07:50.660 --> 00:07:54.400
film tanked, the blame would be on both guys
who don't like female action leads, and the

00:07:54.400 --> 00:07:56.310
entire superhero genre.

00:07:56.310 --> 00:08:00.460
Apparently, even successful female-focused
superhero films like Captain Marvel and Wonder

00:08:00.460 --> 00:08:03.940
Woman, according to Banks, were part of a,
quote, "male genre."

00:08:03.940 --> 00:08:08.120
Right before the movie opened to a downright
dismal box office, she doubled down, saying

00:08:08.120 --> 00:08:11.730
that nobody has complained about all the Spider-Man
movies that have hit the box office in the

00:08:11.730 --> 00:08:16.560
past 20 years, like, say, the three Elizabeth
Banks was in back in the 2000s.

00:08:16.560 --> 00:08:20.860
Of course, that comparison conveniently ignores
the fact that Spidey is a beloved cultural

00:08:20.870 --> 00:08:25.590
icon, while Charlie's Angels is mostly known
as a cheesy '70s television show which was

00:08:25.590 --> 00:08:27.540
never terribly highly-regarded.

00:08:27.540 --> 00:08:32.419
Despite the 2019 film's capable leads, it
seems like moviegoers simply had no use for

00:08:32.420 --> 00:08:34.280
another Charlie's Angels movie.

00:08:34.280 --> 00:08:38.300
They're not likely to get another one, either
as the flick's paltry $8 million dollar opening

00:08:38.300 --> 00:08:42.320
weekend qualified it as a candidate for one
of the year's biggest money-losers.

00:08:43.160 --> 00:08:46.500
Fox's X-Men series wasn't supposed to end
this way.

00:08:46.510 --> 00:08:50.990
Dark Phoenix was supposed to be epic, a proper
adaptation of one of the most iconic storylines

00:08:50.990 --> 00:08:55.320
in all of Marvel comics history, one that
fans were ready to give a second chance after

00:08:55.320 --> 00:08:59.840
it was already botched pretty badly in 2006's
X-Men: The Last Stand.

00:08:59.840 --> 00:09:04.800
Since Disney acquired Fox's television and
film assets with their 2019 merger, the X-Men

00:09:04.800 --> 00:09:08.831
seem destined to enter the Marvel Cinematic
Universe at some point, but Fox could have

00:09:08.831 --> 00:09:12.860
given their series, which ran for two decades
and twelve films, the kind of mind-bending

00:09:12.860 --> 00:09:14.290
sendoff it deserved.

00:09:14.290 --> 00:09:15.820
So what went wrong?

00:09:15.820 --> 00:09:17.540
It wasn't just one thing.

00:09:17.540 --> 00:09:22.290
The flick's third act famously had to be extensively
retooled due to similarities to Captain Marvel,

00:09:22.290 --> 00:09:26.290
and much of the cast, including star Sophie
Turner, were rightly accused of phoning in

00:09:26.290 --> 00:09:29.880
their lackluster performances by the critics
that lambasted the film.

00:09:30.160 --> 00:09:31.920
"And you couldn't even do that.

00:09:36.020 --> 00:09:38.700
Let me show you how."

00:09:38.700 --> 00:09:42.580
Dark Phoenix was supposed to be final entry
in a franchise that had been producing diminishing

00:09:42.589 --> 00:09:44.210
returns for years.

00:09:44.210 --> 00:09:49.779
While 2014's X-Men: Days of Future Past successfully
traded in nostalgia by merging the older and

00:09:49.779 --> 00:09:55.200
younger mutants, 2016's X-Men: Apocalypse
was far less inspired, annoying critics and

00:09:55.200 --> 00:09:59.340
underperforming at the box office, setting
up Dark Phoenix for an epic failure.

00:09:59.340 --> 00:10:04.000
Perhaps if the film had simply been better,
it could have avoided its fate, but it was,

00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:05.720
unfortunately, simply terrible.

00:10:06.260 --> 00:10:10.200
What do you get when you put Matthew McConaughey
and Snoop Dogg in the same stoner comedy?

00:10:10.200 --> 00:10:15.240
Apparently not money, because The Beach Bum
racked up only $1.8 million on its opening

00:10:15.240 --> 00:10:18.600
weekend, the lowest opening gross of McConaughey's
career.

00:10:18.600 --> 00:10:23.220
The film is based on writer/director Harmony
Korine's experiences filming Spring Breakers.

00:10:23.230 --> 00:10:27.760
McConaughey stars as stoner poet Moondog as
he wanders through a smoky life in the Florida

00:10:27.760 --> 00:10:32.680
Keys, which mostly amounts to listening to
a lot of Jimmy Buffet until tragedy strikes.

00:10:32.680 --> 00:10:37.420
Isla Fisher co-stars as Moondog's wife, Minnie,
who finances her husband's shenanigans while

00:10:37.420 --> 00:10:41.440
she has an affair with Moondog's friend Lingerie,
played by Snoop Dogg.

00:10:41.440 --> 00:10:45.480
While assessing numbers for The Beach Bum's
opening weekend, Deadline made a point suggesting

00:10:45.480 --> 00:10:49.620
the film's poor performance might have more
to do with demographics than taste.

00:10:49.620 --> 00:10:53.860
Recalling that Korine's previous film, Spring
Breakers enjoyed a stronger opening, the article

00:10:53.860 --> 00:10:58.750
points out that film's cast, including James
Franco, Selena Gomez, and Vanessa Hudgens,

00:10:58.750 --> 00:11:03.010
had a much stronger cast for a millennial
audience than one with the 50-year-old McConaughey

00:11:03.010 --> 00:11:06.060
and a rapper whose star shone brightest in
the '90s.

00:11:06.060 --> 00:11:10.480
Then again, a plot that's repetitive at best
and depressing at its worst doesn't help matters

00:11:10.480 --> 00:11:11.620
much either.

